      "text": "We begin by noting that $AB$ is a diameter and $O$ is the center of the circle. Given that $\\angle BAE = 43^\\circ$, we aim to find $\\angle CED$.\n\nFirst, we recognize that $AB$ is a diameter, making $\\angle ABE$ a right angle (90 degrees) because the tangent at a point on a circle is perpendicular to the radius at that point. Thus, $\\angle ABE = 90^\\circ$.\n\nNext, we look at $\\angle ABE = 90^\\circ$ and $\\angle BAE = 43^\\circ$. Therefore, we have:\n\\[\n\\angle EAB = 90^\\circ - 43^\\circ = 47^\\circ.\n\\]\n\nNow, considering triangle $BCE$ and using the fact that $BC$ is perpendicular to the tangent at $B$, we know that $\\angle BCE = 90^\\circ$.\n\nSimilarly, in triangle $AED$, we know that $\\angle AED = 90^\\circ$.\n\nTo find $\\angle CED$, we observe the relationships in the quadrilateral $CEDB$. Since $O$ is the center, $OB = OE$ (radii of the circle). This makes $\\angle OBE = 90^\\circ$.\n\nNow, considering the angles around point $B$ and $E$:\n\\[\n\\angle OBE + \\angle OEB = 90^\\circ,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\angle OEB = \\angle OEC = 90^\\circ - \\angle ABE = 90^\\circ - 47^\\circ = 43^\\circ.\n\\]\n\nUsing these, we find $\\angle OEC$:\n\\[\n\\angle OEC = \\angle OEB + \\angle EBC = 43^\\circ + 47^\\circ = 90^\\circ.\n\\]\n\nThus, $\\angle OEC = 90^\\circ$. Therefore, $\\angle CED$ is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{47}\n\\]",
